WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:04.010
Today, woman around the globe

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:07.873
have less access to power,
wealth, and education than men.

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:13.080
But one tiny island is leading the world

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.490
in bridging these gaps.

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:17.610
Iceland is pioneering ways

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:19.453
to get more mothers back to work,

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:22.563
to root out gender stereotypes,

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:25.330
and to close the pay gap.

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:27.120
It's a human rights issue, isn't it?

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:30.620
Paying the same wage for
equally valuable jobs.

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:32.850
Could Iceland inspire the world to solve

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:34.723
one of it's greatest problems?

00:00:45.123 --> 00:00:47.310
Iceland has topped
gender equality rankings

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.150
for nearly a decade.

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:53.280
One of the secrets to
their success: start early.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.920
This kindergarten in
the capital of Reykjavik

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:59.150
focuses on challenging
extreme gender stereotypes

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:01.513
before they take root in boys and girls.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:33.150
It's a mission that's
led to the creation of

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:36.270
17 schools across this tiny country.

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:38.590
All focused on developing
a healthy balance of

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:41.420
characteristics in both sexes.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:43.410
Girls and boys are separated

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:45.020
to allow girls to nurture traits

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:47.070
traditionally viewed as masculine,

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:48.253
like being bold,

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:51.433
independent,

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:56.103
and taking risks.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.950
And boys are given time to
learn traits traditionally

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:03.260
viewed as feminine.

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:05.470
Like being more group oriented,

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:07.240
empathetic,

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:08.153
and caring.

00:02:09.431 --> 00:02:11.980
And the signs are that this is working.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:14.770
Research suggests that in later years,

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:17.590
children from this school have
a greater understanding of

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:20.010
gender equality when compared to children

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:21.183
from other schools.

00:02:41.656 --> 00:02:45.020
Iceland is also promoting
gender equality by encouraging

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:47.863
fathers to share the
childcare burden with mothers.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:54.000
In 2000, it introduced what
is known as a Daddy Quota.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.433
Three months Statutory Paternity Leave.

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:02.960
It's an allowance that
goes much further than

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:04.823
most other countries in the world.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:12.030
Here, over 70 percent of fathers

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:14.163
take up the full 3 months leave.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:15.833
Why?

00:03:15.833 --> 00:03:17.400
Because the state covers
80 percent of his salary

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:22.173
during this period, up to
a cap of $4,600 a month.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:26.520
One beneficiary of this generous system

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:29.973
is Igor Bianason, who's
looking after his son Vala.

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:35.160
Igor believes the high
cost of the Daddy Quota

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.330
to tax payers is justified because

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:39.430
it helps get more women in to work.

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:05.090
But even in Iceland,

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:07.410
men are still paid nearly 6 percent more

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:09.083
than women for similar work.

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:14.270
This year, Iceland became the
first country in the world

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:17.320
to pass legislation, not just to expose,

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:20.100
but to tackle the gender pay gap.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:24.130
Companies with over 25
employees, like Reykjavik Energy,

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:26.430
now have to prove they
are paying men and women

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:28.183
equally for similar jobs.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:31.500
Every job at the company must be measured

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:33.550
against a set of criteria.

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:35.033
This produces a score.

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:38.200
For jobs with the same score,

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:40.133
workers must be paid the same.

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:45.000
When Reykjavik Energy
used this pay calculator,

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:48.193
the inequalities came into
sharp and immediate focus.

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:52.210
We noticed that there was
a pay gap there between the

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:54.400
unskilled workers that were outside and

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:56.760
the unskilled workers inside.

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:00.710
The outside unskilled
workers are mainly men and

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:02.680
the unskilled workers inside,

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:05.480
that's the cleaning staff,
the staff in the kitchen,

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:07.430
that's mostly women.

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:10.450
What's important to keep in
mind is the gender pay gap.

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:13.760
It's not there because
there's a couple of evil men

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:16.200
making decisions to pay women less.

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:19.220
It's this unconscious
bias that we all have.

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:22.893
We place more value on
traditionally male dominated jobs.

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:25.410
The company rectified this by

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:28.780
raising the wages of
it's female employees.

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:31.550
Critics of the law point out
there will be significant

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:33.540
financial consequences for companies

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:36.600
as they rectify their pay inequalities.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:40.530
But many argue this is a
necessary price to pay.

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:42.310
It's a human rights issue, isn't it?

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:45.920
Paying the same wage for
equally valuable jobs.

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:48.970
Having a law that requires
companies to have this,

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:51.453
it makes everyone accountable.

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:54.840
Gender equality will be an
ever more pressing challenge

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:57.570
for wealthy countries across the world.

00:05:57.570 --> 00:06:00.260
Could the ambitious measures
being tested in Iceland

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:01.660
provide practical solutions?

